{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["And the reason of <b>in his day you shall give him his hire, and the sun shall not set upon him</b> — by way of the plain sense, it is an explanation of what is stated in the Torah (Leviticus 19:13): לֹא תָלִין פְּעֻלַּת שָׂכִיר אִתְּךָ עַד בֹּקֶר (\"the wages of a hired worker shall not remain with you overnight until morning\"), for it is the way of Scripture to speak in the present case, and the custom is to hire a laborer for one day, and at evening he departs before sunset, and Scripture commands to pay him on his day — immediately upon completion of his work — and that the sun should not set upon him, so that he may acquire with his wage for himself, his wife, and his children what they will eat at night, for he is poor, as are most hired workers, and it is upon this wage that he sets his life, that he may acquire with it food to sustain his life. It teaches us here that what the Torah says, לֹא תָלִין פְּעֻלַּת שָׂכִיר אִתְּךָ עַד בֹּקֶר, the intent is that you pay him on his day, for if you do not pay him immediately upon his departure from his work, behold he goes to his house and his wage remains with you until morning, and he may die of hunger during the night. And similarly, כִּי הִיא כְּסוּתֹה לְבַדָּהּ הִיא שִׂמְלָתוֹ לְעֹרוֹ בַּמֶּה יִשְׁכָּב (Exodus 22:26) — it mentions the reason of the commandment in most of the prohibitions. And our Sages explained (Bava Metzi'a 110a) that the verse in the Torah <b>עַד בֹּקֶר</b> refers to a day laborer, and it came to explain here the law of a night laborer, and behold each one has a time for payment of twelve hours. And this is the truth in the received tradition and the fitting with a correct reason:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:24.289402244Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}